The `if (percentileBlock.getValueCount(p) != 1)` condition is checked twice. The second check could be removed to avoid redundancy.
```java
int valueCount = percentileBlock.getValueCount(p);
if (valueCount != 1) {
    if (valueCount > 1) {
        warnings.registerException(new IllegalArgumentException("single-value function encountered multi-value"));
    }
    result.appendNull();
    continue position;
}
```

In the `processDoubles`, `processInts`, and `processLongs` methods, there is a redundant check for `percentile == 0` and `percentile == 100` after sorting the values. The checks before sorting are sufficient and can be removed to avoid unnecessary operations.
```java
if (valuesBlock.mvSortedAscending()) {
    if (percentile == 0) {
        builder.appendDouble(valuesBlock.getDouble(0));
    } else if (percentile == 100) {
        builder.appendDouble(valuesBlock.getDouble(valueCount - 1));
    } else {
        assert lowerIndex >= 0 && upperIndex < valueCount;
        var result = calculateDoublePercentile(fraction, valuesBlock.getDouble(lowerIndex), valuesBlock.getDouble(upperIndex));
        builder.appendDouble(result);
    }
} else {
    var values = new double[valueCount];
    for (int i = 0; i < valueCount; i++) {
        values[i] = valuesBlock.getDouble(firstValueIndex + i);
    }
    Arrays.sort(values);
    assert lowerIndex >= 0 && upperIndex < valueCount;
    var result = calculateDoublePercentile(fraction, values[lowerIndex], values[upperIndex]);
    builder.appendDouble(result);
}
```
